<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Theban Revolt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/206-bce">206</a>–186 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Theban Revolt was an uprising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebaid">Thebaid</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic dynasty</a>, lasting from 206 to 186 BCE. Driven by heavy taxes and a devalued currency after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-syrian-war">Fourth Syrian War</a>, Theban autonomist feeling and anti-Greek sentiment, the rebels seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-edfu">Temple of Edfu</a> and Thebes, and their leader was crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> as Haronnophris in 205 BCE. Ptolemy V's army retook Thebes in 199 BCE, but a successor, Chaonnophris, fought on until his defeat south of Aswan in 186 BCE and his execution at Memphis. The revolt pushed later Ptolemies to court the Egyptian clergy and play the role of pharaoh, though a Greco-Macedonian elite still dominated.</p></div>
